    *   *Pros:* Yields a significantly higher deduction, which will lower both your income tax and your self-employment tax.
    *   *Cons:* Requires you to track and keep records of all your utility and rent payments.

**Recommendation:** Use the Actual Expenses method. It will save you substantially more money.

### 4. What Forms or Worksheets Will You Need?
When you file, your tax return will consist of the standard **Form 1040**, plus the following schedules and forms:

*   **Schedule 1 (Additional Income and Adjustments to Income):** This is where you will report your business income (carried over from Schedule C) and claim your $2,500 Student Loan Interest deduction.
*   **Schedule 2 (Additional Taxes):** This is where you will calculate and report the Self-Employment tax (Medicare and Social Security) owed on your freelance income.
*   **Schedule B (Interest and Ordinary Dividends):** Because your combined interest ($2,340) and dividends ($440) exceed $1,500, you are required to fill out Schedule B to list the payers (Chase and Robinhood).
*   **Schedule C (Profit or Loss from Business):** You will use this to report your $11,200 freelance income and deduct your $720 in work supplies.
*   **Form 8829 (Expenses for Business Use of Your Home):** If you choose the Actual Expenses method for your home office, you will fill this out to calculate the exact deduction, which then flows onto your Schedule C.
*   **Schedule SE (Self-Employment Tax):** Used to calculate the exact amount of Social Security and Medicare tax you owe on your net freelance profit. 

*Tip: Don't forget to look out for your 1099-INT from Chase, 1099-DIV from Robinhood, 1098-E from EdFinancial, and any 1099-NECs from your freelance clients!*
